!deck: Math::Real-Numbers
!tags: Math Real-Numbers Absolute-Value Inequality
!type: Basic Math

!id: C3mD0XhGrY
!front: Define the absolute value \( |x| \) of a real number \( x \).
!back:
\[
|x| =
\begin{cases}
    x, & x \ge 0, \\
   -x, & x < 0.
\end{cases}
\]

So \( |x| \) is the nonnegative magnitude of \( x \), ignoring its sign.
~~~

!id: nQgWncXCnf
!front: Explain why \( -|x| \le x \le |x| \).
!back:
If \( x \) is non-negative, then \( |x| = x \), so \( -|x| = -x \le x \le x = |x| \).

If \( x \) is negative, then \( |x| = -x \), so \( -|x| = x \le x \le -x = |x| \).
~~~

!id: mkbLig7hww
!front: Explain why if \( |x| \le c \), \( c \ge 0 \) is true then \( x \le c \) is true as well.
!back:
If \( x \) is non-negative, then \( |x| = x \), so \( x \le c \).

If \( x \) is negative, then \( |x| = -x \), so \( -x \le c \) which implies \( x \ge -c \). Since \( c \ge 0 \), we have \( -c \le c \), so \( x \le c \).
~~~

!id: OFMTtrXppe
!front: Explain why if \( x \le c \), \( c \ge 0 \) is true then \( |x| \le c \) is not necessarily true as well.
!back: Counterexample: Let \( x = -2 \), \( |x| = 2 \) and \( c = 1 \). Then \( -2 \le 1 \) is true, but \( 2 \not \le 1 \).
~~~

!id: kffGeUSWDK
!front: Solve \( |x| \le c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[ -c \le x \le c \]
\[ x \in [-c, c] \]
~~~

!id: um6DlBSRjb
!front: Solve \( |x| \ge c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[ x \le -c \text{ or } x \ge c \]
\[ x \in \left( -\infty, -c \right] \cup \left[ c, \infty \right) \]
~~~

!id: B8fZ2E9XKm
!front: Solve \( |x - a| \le c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[ -c \le x - a \le c \]
\[ a - c \le x \le a + c \]
\[ x \in [a - c, a + c] \]
~~~

!id: H7Z0dQ5N2m
!front: Solve \( |x - a| \ge c \), \( c \ge 0 \).
!back:
\[ x - a \le -c \quad \text{or} \quad x - a \ge c \]
\[ x \le a - c \quad \text{or} \quad x \ge a + c \]
\[ x \in (-\infty, a-c] \cup [a+c, \infty) \]
~~~

!id: T8cM3K6n5A
!front: True or false: For real \( a,b \), \( |a| \le |b| \) implies \( a^2 \le b^2 \).
!back:
True. Squaring preserves the order of nonnegative real numbers, so

\[ |a| \le |b| \Rightarrow |a|^2 \le |b|^2 \Rightarrow a^2 \le b^2 \]
~~~

!id: kxxCWMfyuv
!front: If \( a^2 \le b^2 \), what inequality does this imply about \( |a| \) and \( |b| \)?
!back: \[ |a| \le |b| \]
~~~

!id: rPac9OHGod
!front: Prove the inequality \( ab \le |a| |b| \).
!back:
- Case 1: \( a,b \ge 0 \). Then \( |a|=a \), \( |b|=b \), so \( ab=|a||b| \).
- Case 2: \( a,b \le 0 \). Then \( |a|=-a \), \( |b|=-b \), so \( ab=(-a)(-b)=|a||b| \).
- Case 3: \( a \) and \( b \) have opposite signs. Say \( a \ge 0 \), \( b \le 0 \). Then \( ab \le 0 \), but \( |a||b| \ge 0 \). So automatically \( ab \le |a||b| \). Same if \( a \le 0 \), \( b \ge 0 \).
~~~

!id: OYERfFZfqi
!front: State the triangle inequality for real numbers.
!back:
For all real numbers \( a \) and \( b \), \[ |a+b| \le |a|+|b|. \]

It says the magnitude of a sum is at most the sum of the magnitudes.
~~~

!id: L38GkTkAdr
!front: Prove the triangle inequality for real numbers directly from the definition of absolute value.
!back:
Let:

- \( a,b\in\mathbb{R} \) and \( M=|a|+|b|\ge 0 \).

To prove: \( |a+b|\le M \).

- The definition of absolute value gives \( -|a|\le a\le |a| \) and \( -|b|\le b\le |b| \).
- Adding the lower and upper bounds gives \( -M\le a+b\le M \).
- Since \( M\ge 0 \), the double inequality is equivalent to \( |a+b|\le M=|a|+|b| \).
~~~

!id: Y7atRaR4qL
!front: State the reverse triangle inequality for real numbers.
!back:
For all real numbers \( a \) and \( b \), \[ \bigl||a|-|b|\bigr| \le |a-b|. \]

It says the distance between magnitudes is at most the distance between the original numbers.
~~~

!id: eorfBwq4bk
!front: Prove the reverse triangle inequality for real numbers using the triangle inequality.
!back:
Let:

- \( a,b\in\mathbb{R} \) and \( d=|a-b|\ge 0 \).

To prove: \( \bigl||a|-|b|\bigr|\le d \).

- Since \( a=(a-b)+b \), the triangle inequality gives \( |a|\le d+|b| \), hence \( |a|-|b|\le d \).
- Since \( b=(b-a)+a \) and \( |b-a|=d \), it also gives \( |b|-|a|\le d \).
- Together these say \( -d\le |a|-|b|\le d \), which is equivalent to \( \bigl||a|-|b|\bigr|\le d=|a-b| \).
~~~

!id: CWAazZMbtb
!front: Prove that \( |a - b| \le |a| + |b| \) for any real \( a, b \).
!back:
Apply the triangle inequality with \( b \) replaced by \( -b \):

\[
\begin{align*}
|a - b| &= |a + (-b)| \\
        &\le |a| + |-b| \\
        &= |a| + |b|
\end{align*}
\]
~~~

!id: yAqWUYuXhd
!front: Prove that \( \big| |a| - |b| \big| \le |a + b| \) for any real \( a, b \).
!back:
Apply the reverse triangle inequality with \( b \) replaced by \( -b \):

\[ \big| |a| - |-b| \big| \le |a - (-b)| \]

Since \( |-b| = |b| \) and \( a - (-b) = a + b \), this becomes

\[ \big| |a| - |b| \big| \le |a + b| \]
~~~

!id: 8dY5wY36R2
!front: Prove that \( |a| \le |a - b| + |b| \) for any real \( a, b \).
!back:
Write \( a = (a-b) + b \), then apply the triangle inequality:

\[ |a| = |(a-b)+b| \le |a-b| + |b| \]
~~~

!id: GGbwzkEeEu
!front: Prove that \( |a+b+c| \le |a| + |b| + |c| \) for any real \( a, b, c \).
!back:
Apply the triangle inequality twice, first to \( (a+b) \) and \( c \), then to \( a \) and \( b \):

\[
\begin{align*}
|a+b+c| &= |(a+b)+c| \\
        &\le |a+b| + |c| \\
        &\le |a| + |b| + |c|
\end{align*}
\]
~~~

!id: P9KMLP4z9B
!front: If \( |x-a| < \delta \) and \( |y-b| < \varepsilon \), how can you bound \( |(x+y)-(a+b)| \)?
!back:
Use the triangle inequality:
\[
\begin{align*}
    |(x+y)-(a+b)|
        &= |(x-a)+(y-b)| \\
        &\le |x-a|+|y-b| \\
        &< \delta+\varepsilon.
\end{align*}
\]
~~~

!id: RZhId0Cdoh
!front: If \( |x| \le M \) and \( |x-y| < \varepsilon \), how can you bound \( |y| \)?
!back: Use \( y=x+(y-x) \) and the triangle inequality: \[ |y| \le |x|+|y-x| < M+\varepsilon. \]
~~~
